@page "/HowItWorks/futures"
@using InvestmentTaxCalculator.Components

<HowItWorksGuideLayout PageAddress="HowItWorks/futures">
    <ChildContent>
        <div class="section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Long and short positions</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    An outstanding futures contract is treated as an asset (TCGA 1992 s143). Selling a contract to open a
                    position is an <em>acquisition</em> of a short contract, not a disposal.
                </li>
                <li>
                    The app goes through each contract's trades in date order and tracks your open position, so it can label
                    each trade as opening or closing a long or short position. A single trade that closes a short and opens a
                    long, or the other way round, is split into two.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Short positions are a separate asset, shown with "Short " in front of the contract name. Both long and
                    short positions use the same <a href="HowItWorks/same-day-rule" class="link-styled">matching rules</a> as
                    shares.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="section">
            <h2 class="section-title">Cost and gain</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    No money changes hands for the contract itself when you open a position, so the acquisition cost is only
                    the dealing costs.
                </li>
                <li>
                    The app records the contract value of each trade, and keeps it in the Section 104 pool alongside the cost.
                    The profit or loss is the difference between the contract values of the selling and buying sides.
                </li>
                <li>A profit is added to the disposal proceeds, and a loss is added to the allowable cost.</li>
                <li>
                    Dealing costs are converted to pounds at the rate on the day each one was charged. The profit or loss is
                    converted at the rate of the trade that closes the position.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Margin payments are deposits, not payments for the contract (CG56060), so they do not change the
                    calculation.
                </li>
                <li>Futures gains and losses are reported under "Other property, assets and gains".</li>
            </ul>
            <div class="alert alert-info alert-custom" role="note">
                Settlement by delivery is not supported. The app also does not check whether a contract is traded on a
                recognised futures exchange, which can affect how it is taxed.
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card mb-4">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h2 class="h5 card-title">Worked example</h2>
                <p>
                    You buy one contract with a contract value of $50,000, paying $5 in commission, and later sell it at a
                    contract value of $52,000, paying another $5. Assume both fees convert to £4 each, and the rate on the
                    closing trade is £0.80 per dollar.
                </p>
                <p class="mb-0">
                    The contract profit is ($52,000 − $50,000) × 0.80 = £1,600, which becomes the disposal proceeds. The
                    allowable cost is the two fees, £8, giving a <strong>gain of £1,592</strong>.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </ChildContent>
    <FurtherReading>
        <li><a href="https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/1992/12/section/143" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">TCGA 1992 s143</a>: commodity and financial futures</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.gov.uk/hmrc-internal-manuals/capital-gains-manual/cg56060" class="link-styled" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HMRC CG56060</a>: futures margin payments</li>
    </FurtherReading>
</HowItWorksGuideLayout>
